CHARACTER ARCS — SEASON 1

NAËL CORTI (20) — Played by [Actor TBD]

Season Arc: From concealment to curiosity to conscious control to acceptance—but acceptance requires sacrifice.

Internal Journey:
Naël begins the season believing his skin is a disease to be managed. He has built his entire life around concealment: emotional control, physical isolation, the dream of the open ocean where no one knows him. The season forces him to confront three truths in sequence:

1. The skin is not a disease. It is a living system that integrated with his body. (Episodes 3–5)
2. The skin responds to desire. His control is not discipline but suppression. The skin wants what he wants. (Episodes 6–8)
3. The skin is not separate from him. It is him. The question is not whether to accept it, but what acceptance costs. (Episodes 9–10)

Key Physical/Emotional Beats:

· Episode 1: Rescue under adrenaline—involuntary texture change, witnesses, Thalassia reactivation.
· Episode 3: Léa sees him change, asks if it hurts, he lies. First active choice: the lie as self-protection.
· Episode 4: Voluntary experimentation—first conscious pigmentation shift, immediate metabolic cost, he does it again anyway. This is the moment he becomes the protagonist of his own story.
· Episode 6: Near-total camouflage while protecting Yanis—the most intense use of the system yet, metabolic collapse.
· Episode 8: Public partial revelation—he chooses to be seen to save lives.
· Episode 9: First Adaptive Morphogenesis—the body negotiating with physics.
· Episode 10: Final acceptance—the skin is his, he is not hiding, he is not showing, he is being.

What He Loses:
The marine-rescue certification dream. The possibility of anonymity. Léa's uncomplicated love. The town he grew up in. His legal identity as "normal."

---

ÉLIAS CORTI (52) — Father

Season Arc: From protective secrecy to partial confession to a final choice between his son's safety and his son's autonomy.

Internal Journey:
Élias has spent nine years lying—to his son, to his wife, to himself. He told himself the lie was protection: if Naël never knew what he was, he could never be found. But the lie has cost him his relationship with his son, his career, and his peace of mind.

The season forces Élias to confront the truth: the lie was never about protecting Naël. It was about protecting himself—from guilt, from responsibility, from the moment he chose to hide the accident rather than seek help.

Key Beats:

· Episode 2: Flashback—the accident, the call not to emergency services but to Farah Ilan.
· Episode 4: Élias retrieves the frozen AMB culture, considers destroying it, does not.
· Episode 5: The confrontation—Élias admits the partial truth but withholds Naël's active role in the breach.
· Episode 7: Samia's choice to treat Naël herself forces Élias to confront how much he has asked his family to hide.
· Episode 9: Final revelation—Élias tells Naël everything, including the fact that Naël triggered the breach.
· Episode 10: Élias must choose whether to help Naël fight or to try to "cure" him. He chooses to help—but the choice costs him.

What He Loses:
The fiction of himself as a protector. His son's trust (even as it is partially rebuilt, it is never the same). Possibly his freedom—if Thalassia pursues legal action.

---

SAMIA CORTI (49) — Mother

Season Arc: From controlling love to a choice between safety and autonomy.

Internal Journey:
Samia has known for nine years that something changed in her son. She did not know the scientific truth, but she knew the clinical signs: the skin changes, the metabolic crashes, the way Naël learned to suppress emotion before he learned to suppress anything else. Her response was to control—diet, environment, stress, exposure. Her love was surveillance.

The season forces Samia to confront the limits of control. She cannot protect Naël from what he is. She can only choose whether to help him become who he is becoming.

Key Beats:

· Episode 1: Samia notices Naël's fatigue, tries to control his environment, he resists.
· Episode 3: Samia observes the changes but says nothing—her silence is complicity.
· Episode 5: Samia learns about Thalassia's presence and confronts Élias privately. She knows more than she has admitted.
· Episode 7: The turning point—Naël collapses, Samia discovers the full truth, and instead of hospitalization, she treats him herself. She chooses his autonomy over his safety.
· Episode 9: Samia helps Naël escape Thalassia's encirclement, using her nursing knowledge to stabilize him after Adaptive Morphogenesis.
· Episode 10: Samia's final choice—she tells Naël to go, to leave Sables-d'Or, even though it means she may never see him again.

What She Loses:
The illusion that she could keep him safe. Her son's presence in her daily life. Possibly her marriage—the strain of the season's revelations is enormous.

