DR. FARAH ILAN (58) — Former AMB Scientist

Season Arc: From guarded secrecy to final revelation to sacrifice.

Internal Journey:
Farah is dying. The early AMB exposure that gave her unstable abilities also triggered early-onset neurological degeneration. She is running out of time, and she knows it. Her entire arc is a race against her own body: can she tell Naël the truth before she loses the capacity to tell anyone anything?

Farah is also the only person who truly understands what Naël is. She was there when the AMB system was designed, when it failed, when it was hidden. She carries the guilt of the program's consequences—and the hope that Naël might be the success they never achieved.

Key Beats:

· Episode 2: Farah refuses to speak to Diane Rocher—but her refusal is not silence. It is protection.
· Episode 5: Farah shows Naël the AMB archives, explains Engineered Living Materials, tells him about Yanis Reba.
· Episode 6: Farah reveals the energy constraint—the AMB system as a second metabolism.
· Episode 7: Farah explains that she has an unstable AMB exposure herself—her degeneration is slowed by the same system that is killing her.
· Episode 9: Farah reveals the theoretical ceiling: Adaptive Morphogenesis. She also reveals the name—MIMESIS—for the first time.
· Episode 10: In the finale, Farah records a final message—the voice-over that pronounces MIMESIS. It is unclear whether she survives the season.

What She Loses:
Possibly her life. Certainly her guarded privacy. The last years of her career, spent hiding rather than researching.

---

YANIS REBA (34) — "The Fisherman"

Season Arc: From mysterious observer to radical philosopher to an open-ended fate.

Internal Journey:
Yanis is what Naël could become—not in a simple "dark mirror" sense, but in a deeper philosophical sense. Yanis has spent years without family, without support, without the emotional suppression that Naël developed. His skin changes constantly, unpredictably, painfully. He has learned that the AMB system responds to desire—and his desires are vast, unfulfilled, and increasingly radical.

Yanis's philosophy: the AMB system is not a disease to be managed but a gift to be embraced. The world should see them. Hiding is what makes them specimens. Visibility is power.

He approaches Naël not as an enemy but as a potential ally—someone who shares his condition and might share his vision. The tragedy is that Naël cannot accept this vision, even as he cannot entirely reject it.

Key Beats:

· Episode 3: First appearance—a silhouette watching Naël from the breakwater.
· Episode 6: Full introduction—Yanis's story, his philosophy, his desperate desire to understand how Naël achieved equilibrium.
· Episode 7: Yanis carries the collapsed Naël away from Thalassia's failed capture attempt.
· Episode 8: Yanis opposes Thalassia's manipulation violently—but argues that visibility is the answer. He and Naël clash philosophically.
· Episode 9: Yanis's instability reaches critical mass. He is right about one thing: Naël's control is built on suppression.
· Episode 10: Yanis escapes during the finale. Naël lets him go. His fate remains open—redemption, relapse, or radicalization.

What He Loses:
The possibility of a community with Naël (at least for now). His physical stability (already fragile, now further damaged). His freedom (he is now a fugitive).

