EPISODE 2: "THE CENTER"

Logline: A trip to the abandoned research center reveals the origins of Naël's condition—and the accident that changed everything.

Cold Open (Past Timeline — Age 11):
The Sables-d'Or Marine Research Center, nine years ago. Élias works in a laboratory filled with tanks, monitors, and biological cultures. Young Naël visits, supposedly to see where his father works. He is told not to enter the growth lab. But the door is open. He sees the bioluminescence. He enters.

Main Title.

Act One (Present Timeline):
Naël experiences extreme, unexplained fatigue. Every physical exertion now has a price. He is eating constantly, sleeping poorly, and his mother has noticed. Samia tries to control his environment—more rest, different foods, less stress—but Naël resists. He is an adult. He does not want to be managed.

At night, Naël secretly researches the AMB program. The references are fragmentary: a marine bio-engineering initiative, closed under unclear circumstances, its lead scientist—Élias Corti—leaving "for personal reasons." Naël realizes the program was his father's work.

He visits the abandoned research center. The building is deteriorating, but he finds the old laboratory. The growth tanks are empty, the equipment stripped. But he finds something: a child's drawing, preserved in a frame on what was once his father's desk. It shows an octopus and a boy, colored in bright blues and greens. Young Naël's artwork.

Act Two:
Naël confronts his father. Élias deflects, offering the same line he has used for nine years: "You have a rare condition. We manage it." Naël pushes harder. "What was AMB? What were you working on?" Élias refuses to answer. The confrontation ends with Naël storming out.

Flashback (Central Episode):
The growth lab, nine years ago. Young Naël stands before the culture tank, transfixed by the bioluminescence. The organisms inside are beautiful—living light, pulsing and shifting. He reaches out. His hand touches the glass.

The tank cracks.

The culture escapes, a luminous flood. Naël scrambles back, but the material is already on his skin, seeking entry through a small cut on his hand. He does not scream. He watches, fascinated and terrified, as the light sinks into his body.

Élias finds him. He sees what has happened. His face shifts through a dozen emotions: horror, guilt, and then—decision. He picks up the phone. Not to call emergency services. To call someone else.

The screen cuts to black.

Act Three:
Present day. Naël, unable to sleep, walks to the cliffs above the ocean. He thinks about the drawing—the octopus and the boy. He wonders what his father was really working on.

Flashback (Hours After the Accident):
Élias does not take Naël to a hospital. He takes him to Farah Ilan's home lab. They monitor him. The skin changes begin within six hours—subtle shifts in texture, color, temperature. Élias makes a decision: "We treat it as a rare dermatological condition. We hide the source."

Young Naël, feverish, overhears fragments: "integration," "rejection," "if it stabilizes." He thinks they are saying he is going to die.

Stinger:
Present day. Diane Rocher arrives in Sables-d'Or under cover—a researcher studying coastal environmental impacts. She begins interviewing former center employees. One of them mentions Dr. Farah Ilan. Diane makes a note.

End of Episode.

---

EPISODE 3: "SABLES-D'OR"

Logline: A party, a lie, and a first kiss—Naël's condition is witnessed by someone new, and Thalassia makes its first move.

Cold Open (Past Timeline — Age 12):
Naël, one year after the accident, sits in a doctor's office. The physician examines him, confused. "The skin condition is stable, but unusual. I've never seen anything like it." Élias, beside him, lies smoothly: "It's genetic. We're managing it." Young Naël watches his father lie, and learns the lesson: hiding is normal.

Main Title.

Act One (Present Timeline):
Naël meets Léa Santini at the marine research station where she is doing her thesis internship. She is bright, curious, and unafraid—everything Naël is not. They connect over their shared love of the ocean. For the first time in years, Naël feels like a person, not a patient.

Diane Rocher interviews Dr. Farah Ilan, who refuses to speak. Diane does not threaten. She offers: "We can fix him. We have funding. We have facilities. Does he have either?" Farah's face is unreadable. "He has something you can't give him," she says. "He has a life."

Act Two:
A beach party. Naël, at Léa's invitation, attends—reluctant, but wanting to see her. The atmosphere is relaxed. Alcohol flows. Naël drinks, against his better judgment. He and Léa talk, laugh, move closer together.

And then it happens. Under alcohol and emotion—the proximity to Léa, the warmth of the evening, the unfamiliar feeling of being wanted—Naël loses control. His skin shifts visibly: pigmentation, texture, a brief but unmistakable transformation.

Léa sees. The people around them do not—too caught up in their own conversations—but Léa sees everything. She does not run. She does not scream. She asks: "Does it hurt?"

Naël lies: "No."

This is his first active choice in their relationship—the lie as self-protection. It is also the first time he has chosen to hide from someone who might have understood.

Act Three:
Naël leaves the party alone, shaking. He walks to the beach, strips down, and wades into the cold water. The salt stings, but it also steadies him. He floats on his back, staring at the stars, and wonders what would have happened if he had told the truth.

Flashback (Age 13):
Naël in school. A classmate notices something odd about his skin—a patch that seems darker in certain light. Naël deflects: "It's a birthmark." The classmate accepts this. But Naël learns another lesson: people see what they expect to see.

Stinger:
A silhouette on the breakwater. A man—The Fisherman—watches Naël float in the water. He does not approach. He studies, as if observing a specimen. His own skin shifts in the moonlight, changing texture and color in waves that seem painful. He does not seem to notice.

End of Episode.

