EPISODE 10: "MIMESIS"

Logline: In the season finale, Naël faces Thalassia, Yanis, and the truth of what he is—and makes a sacrifice that changes everything.

Cold Open (Past Timeline — Age 11, The Night After the Accident):
Young Naël lies in Farah Ilan's home lab, feverish and afraid. His skin shifts beneath the surface, already changing. He whispers to himself: "I am still me. I am still me. I am still me." The words are a prayer, a hope, a desperate wish. The material responds, settling into his body. It heard him. It is trying to help.

Main Title.

Act One (Present Timeline):
The old marine research center. Thalassia's forces have surrounded the area. Naël, Élias, Farah, and Yanis are trapped. Vannier demands surrender: "Come quietly. We can help you. We can cure you."

Naël refuses. "I don't need a cure. I need you to leave me alone."

Vannier's response is patient, paternalistic: "You are dangerous—to yourself and to others. You cannot consent to your own danger. We are trying to help you."

The standoff begins.

Act Two:
Thalassia moves in. The extraction is violent—teams of operatives with containment equipment, weapons, and the confidence of people who believe they are doing the right thing.

Yanis fights—wildly, desperately, his skin shifting in explosive bursts that are as dangerous to himself as to his enemies. Naël tries to protect him, but the situation is spiraling out of control.

Under extreme pressure—life-or-death, not merely dramatic—Naël triggers Adaptive Morphogenesis for the first time. His body does not simply blend with the environment. It negotiates physically with it—becoming rigid against crushing force, permeable against chemical exposure, insulated against thermal shock. He is not invulnerable. He is conversing with physics.

The effect is stunning—and costly. Naël's body is burning through energy at a rate that cannot be sustained. Farah screams at him to stop: "You'll kill yourself!"

But Naël does not stop. He uses the ability to create an opening—for Yanis to escape, for his father to retreat, for Farah to get clear. He fights not for himself but for the people around him.

Flashback (Past Timeline — Age 20, One Month Before the Season):
Naël stands at the cliffs above the ocean. He is thinking about the future—the marine-rescue certification, the possibility of leaving Sables-d'Or, the dream of the open ocean. For the first time in years, he feels something like hope.

And then, unbidden, his skin shifts—a small, almost imperceptible change. He does not notice. But the system is responding to his hope, his desire, his longing. It is trying to give him what he wants.

Act Three:
The battle ends. Thalassia retreats—not defeated, but withdrawn. Vannier has obtained a biological sample despite the day's failure. The path to program replication is open. Naël may no longer be the first, nor the only.

Yanis is not defeated as a simple enemy. His fate remains open—between tragedy and possible redemption—laying groundwork for Season 2. In the chaos, Yanis escapes. Naël lets him go.

The true finale is intimate. Léa comes to the shore. She does not ask for explanation. She asks: "What do you want?"

Naël, for the first time in his life, answers honestly. "I want the ocean. I want to stop hiding. I want to be both things—the boy who looked at the octopus, and the thing he has become."

Léa does not run. She does not cry. She simply nods. "Then go," she says. "Go be both things."

The sacrifice is clear: Naël chooses to leave. He sacrifices the life he built—the town, the certification, the possibility of a normal relationship—for the chance to become what he is.

Final Scene:
Alone, facing the ocean, Naël accepts what he is. Farah Ilan, in voice-over, pronounces the classified program name under which his file was registered at biological birth:

"MIMESIS."

The camera holds on his skin. It does not change. It is simply his. He is not hiding. He is not showing. He is being.

The screen fades to black.

Post-Credits Scene:
A laboratory. The biological sample obtained by Thalassia is being analyzed. A technician looks up from the microscope, pale. "Sir," he says to Vannier, "the sample is... replicating."

Vannier studies the readout. "Of course it is," he says quietly. "That's what it was designed to do."

The camera pushes in on the sample: cells dividing, adapting, evolving. The AMB system is alive. It is learning. And it is no longer confined to one body.

End of Season.

