APPENDIX: THE RULES OF THE AMB SYSTEM

(For the writers' room reference)

Core Principle

The AMB system is not a superpower. It is a parallel metabolism that consumes the host. Every conscious adaptation costs energy immediately.

Metabolic Cost Table

Adaptation Immediate Cost Recovery Time
Pigmentation shift (mild) Mild hypoglycemia, fatigue Hours
Texture change (callus, scale, membrane) Significant caloric debt, tremors 12–24 hours
Full camouflage Metabolic collapse without immediate refueling Days
Adaptive Morphogenesis (local mechanical modification) Theoretical ceiling; near-fatal energy expenditure Weeks (theoretical)

Emotional Component

The skin does not obey commands like a machine. It negotiates—it responds to emotional state, body temperature, and environmental pressure. Control is not technical; it is physiological and psychological.

Crucially, the skin adapts to desire. It responds to what the host wants, not just what the host needs. This means that emotional suppression is a form of control—but it is also a form of self-erasure. The skin wants what the host wants, but it does not understand limits.

The Adaptation Hierarchy

The AMB system operates on a spectrum of complexity:

1. Pigmentation — color change, camouflage (basic)
2. Texture — surface modification (callus, scale, membrane)
3. Full Camouflage — complete environmental blending (advanced)
4. Adaptive Morphogenesis — local mechanical modification (rigidity, elasticity, friction, thermal insulation) (theoretical ceiling)

The Cost of Suppression

Naël's control is built on denial—nine years of not wanting anything too much. This is not equilibrium; it is a dam. The dam will break. The question is what happens when it does.

