DESIGN PATTERN NOTES FOR NETFLIX PRODUCTION

Episode Duration Flexibility

The 10 episodes are designed to run 55–60 minutes each, with natural act breaks at approximately 15, 30, and 45 minutes. The cold opens (past timeline) run 2–3 minutes, establishing thematic questions before the main title.

Binge-Watch Architecture

· Episodes 1–3: Establishment—the world, the condition, the threat. Ends with the first conscious choice (the lie to Léa) and the first appearance of The Fisherman.
· Episodes 4–6: Escalation—experimentation, revelation, collapse. Ends with the metabolic crisis and the beginning of Thalassia's active pursuit.
· Episodes 7–9: Crisis—the price of power, the provocation, the full truth. Ends with the encirclement and the question of what Naël will do.
· Episode 10: Resolution—the sacrifice, the acceptance, the open threads for Season 2.

Visual Signature Moments

Each episode contains at least one "signature moment"—a scene designed to be shared on social media:

· Episode 1: The rescue—Naël's arm transforming underwater as he grabs the drowning teenager.
· Episode 2: The tank crack—bioluminescent material flooding toward young Naël.
· Episode 3: The party—Léa seeing Naël change for the first time.
· Episode 4: The cove—Naël weeping from exhaustion after his first conscious control.
· Episode 5: The safe—Diane photographing the frozen vial.
· Episode 6: The camouflage—Naël disappearing into the environment while protecting Yanis.
· Episode 7: The collapse—Samia discovering the truth about her son.
· Episode 8: The public reveal—Naël rescuing the trapped workers in full daylight.
· Episode 9: Adaptive Morphogenesis—Naël's body negotiating with physics.
· Episode 10: The final shot—Naël facing the ocean, accepting what he is.

